{"title":"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the interior of a square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> we construct the equilateral triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/1/911c4eb6a2f85d522f6dc928c5ca11f197b399cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABK, BCL, CDM, DAN.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"206\" height=\"16\" /> Prove that the midpoints of the four segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/c/feca780cbe3262ebc09df01b60ad44e89fad1e0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL, LM, MN, NK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"155\" height=\"18\" /> and the midpoints of the eight segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba49bdcb2051a81c2b5322033cc132586ca6a955.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AK, BK, BL, CL, CM, DM, DN, AN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"298\" height=\"16\" /> are the 12 vertices of a regular dodecagon.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Just use complex numbers, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48d02444a59b917b64e8bd0963b88712fc7fa0a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/3/3133441836df66c5854a07fdeb18d723fb40a2ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = i$\" width=\"37\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/c/4dcf358c40464666806d97fcd43762c14fe6a28b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c = - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"54\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a93602cb63fb275370288c19b369a71c208d5c5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d = - i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" />.\nWith some calculations, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/0/140205450a9b9974a66461964fe1ca6d8d89aeb1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = \\frac {\\sqrt {3} - 1}{2}( - 1 - i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"158\" height=\"41\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/9/269c1bf4e0f3b99818a1565b94edd72b56b37a5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l = \\frac {\\sqrt {3} - 1}{2}(1 - i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"140\" height=\"41\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/b/41be43b8b79cbc95d6284e5d97866cb9b8657a5e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m = \\frac {\\sqrt {3} - 1}{2}(1 + i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"150\" height=\"41\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/656ea11af679911f5749252f43817636967811a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = \\frac {\\sqrt {3} - 1}{2}( - 1 + i)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"159\" height=\"41\" />.\nNow it's an easy job to calculate the twelve midpoints and to find out they are all of the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/f/92f48f154a2eaf00bb37feffc61c82fbaba34367.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {\\sqrt {3} - 1}{2}e^{\\frac {k\\pi}{6}i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"90\" height=\"41\" />, with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459602e8e049563b0879fa8ce14d861888dc8e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\in\\mathbb{N}: 0\\le k\\le 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"145\" height=\"15\" />, and the result follows.\n</p><p>The above solution was posted and copyrighted by Joao Pedro Santos.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Synthetic solution:\n</p><p>Clearly the four first midpoints form a square with midpoint <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> where\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />.  Hence it suffices to check that the other\neight points have the same distance from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and that the angles at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\nare correct.\n</p><p>By symmetry, it will then suffice to check that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/f/26f3e8fd8279f7b5a49cd55cea3f8493b96e6e36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle OEF$\" width=\"59\" height=\"13\" /> is\nequilateral where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef091d889893c7c10e475a93e4ef626ff70aa5ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KL$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the midpoint\nof <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/370334b1a3582d54fad42c735dfdded7d216d3cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />.  Being midlines in triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/2/78229da3d086d5357941a5a0cccad3e12cfa97f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AKL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/9/da9e27abb3808086cb1126c19634ac61158607ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AKC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"63\" height=\"18\" />,\nwe see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/c/d2ca374dc18473cae6e67787e11e3287396d4fbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF = \\frac{1}{2} AL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a17419504cf6f3e6ebf08fd6d600febfc333ade8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OF = \\frac{1}{2} KC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"99\" height=\"38\" />.  On the other\nhand, in the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/b/c8baa210c35a8d98d01f9f4d4c956c5a7bcd15ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$KLMN$\" width=\"65\" height=\"12\" /> we clearly have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/6/716580ce7a737a3433cba094d9a540f4325b2b75.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EO = \\frac{1}{2} KL$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"97\" height=\"38\" />.  But by\nconstruction, the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/8/398733544fa29b4686795dca1e01d8a8d614bb88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABL, \\triangle LBK, \\triangle KBC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"196\" height=\"18\" />\nare all congruent whence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28928ea870836bee5aa5774717e2dea8925f97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AL = LK = KC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"136\" height=\"14\" />, and hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e38866569ec4c4b70465ab1a813f05b2b861c34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF = EO = OF$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"136\" height=\"14\" /> as\ndesired.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1977_1_1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/7/74/Prob_1977_1_1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"397\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>(This solution was posted by Tintarn on the discussion page mentioned below.\nThis solution (just like the one above) would benefit from the addition of\nsome details, but it is nevertheless a good solution.)\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In this solution we use analytic (coordinate) geometry.  Let us take\nthe origin to be at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and the axes to be along <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/5/ad51f7f8dd234a8382abd2405ed3d6f6148b7c4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />.  We\ncan assume the square <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> has side <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so the four vertices are\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c21c78ed1d9095c30ef4a80af5981614b5b35a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = (0, 0), B = (2, 0), C = (2, 2), D = (0, 2).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"348\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1977_1_2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/e5/Prob_1977_1_2.png/600px-Prob_1977_1_2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"546\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/e/e5/Prob_1977_1_2.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff613a7e70b592fdbba4e9bb89327b7922eb1339.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_k, k = 1, \\dots, 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" /> be the mid points specified in the problem.\n(Note that it seems that the mid points have some additional properties,\nfor example <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/4/674a747a463b02d508301f03fe53647edd03e68f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> seems to be on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/5/4b5b047090daded93b8b605f81fa7706d02f99f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BL$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/a/85a07ccee62fdd7dad2406887b63c4ffd4c6d73b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> seems to be on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/5/1f5d96c8d1e481244e028c9e97d1d7b53206536b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DM$\" width=\"34\" height=\"12\" />, etc.\nWe are not interested in this now, so we will not investigate these\nproperties.)\n</p><p>The plan is to compute the coordinates of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/b/00b3ae44d4e43ee660df8849070f33ad584e8b37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L, M, N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" />, then compute\nthe coordinates of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/6/ff613a7e70b592fdbba4e9bb89327b7922eb1339.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_k, k = 1, \\dots, 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />.  This will enable us to\ncompute the lengths of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/6/c4631d8c03c69e20fbda6fde7c569da9a50607d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1e_2, e_2e_3, \\dots, e_{12}e_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"152\" height=\"13\" />,\nthe slopes of these segments, and the angles between adjacent segments.\nNoticing that the lengths and angles are respectively equal, will have\nsolved the problem.  Note that we don't have to compute all of them.\nBecause of various symmetries, it is enough to compute some of them.\n</p><p>Specifically, we will compute the coordinates of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/9/9f96151e8f20138ca035a6e5bf185a9101f6900b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_3, e_4, e_5, e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"90\" height=\"13\" />,\nthe lengths of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/6/74658321d20afd4cb905d5da73a343b2f5b2c7d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94c62f56d1a924585721c97ecb82b7b610bc9af3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_4e_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"13\" />, the slopes of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/d/85d07b868b8f8fa73a30ab25421335f2eb77db72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5e_6, e_4e_5, e_3e_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"113\" height=\"13\" />, which will let us determine the angles at\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/a/85a07ccee62fdd7dad2406887b63c4ffd4c6d73b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/8/698b4a4aacd0970bba84553de35ec100334afbdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>An easy computation yields that the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/b/00b3ae44d4e43ee660df8849070f33ad584e8b37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L, M, N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"87\" height=\"16\" /> have coordinates\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/e/07e9ffe1e3cac96f8af14de932883590fbc7e3fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K = (1, \\sqrt{3}), L = (2 - \\sqrt{3}, 1), M = (1, 2 - \\sqrt{3}), N = (\\sqrt{3}, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"473\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p><p>Then we have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/5/415977cb9b0ee7fd46f4330161fac6043fe98812.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_3 = \\left( \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right), e_4 = \\left( \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right), e_5 = \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right), e_6 = \\left( \\frac{4 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\frac{1}{2} \\right).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"623\" height=\"55\" />\n</p><p>A trivial computation shows that the squares of the lengths of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b5484d799170adc8332e7685e6ee77c58b74916e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_4e_5, e_5e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" /> are both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/1/6918103cd69c9ed62adee527fbef4008cf5da1b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7 - 4\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now remember that if we have two points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/0/08034aa5d0063bfc2155dc34bf5b1cde5362cdfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P = (p_x, p_y), Q = (q_x, q_y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"199\" height=\"20\" />\nthen\n</p><p>slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/d/f0db86e042072ca3a6b3dcf0ee823ee77fa04b60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ = \\tan($\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"88\" height=\"20\" />of the angle between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab17967324fc2b12349c0610c6c256f4c829162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"18\" /> and the positive\ndirection of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />-axis<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/4/714ce8075730dc518ee7d422971bbb9de63aad48.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$) = \\frac{p_y - q_y}{p_x - q_x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"93\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>In our case, the slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/6/74658321d20afd4cb905d5da73a343b2f5b2c7d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"13\" /> is clearly 0.  A trivial computation\nshows that the slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/e/2ee2b5952a474bf97c985ba7c9d952a5ba6041dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5e_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"13\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/b/17b01186b6543ee89925542472d1b9eefafb768b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\tan 150^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"133\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>A similar computation shows that the slope of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94c62f56d1a924585721c97ecb82b7b610bc9af3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_4e_5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"13\" /> is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/a/6fafcc8b581c467a756530935fbf145fabd86b67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 120^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"129\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>We conclude that the angles at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/c/72c53a19b87165bf1dc62cf33fe4a3b77772dd66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_5, e_4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> are both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/5/995f44efd3d9791d3844223f8bd6b35c600050fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$150^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />, as\nexpected (since the angles of a regular dodecagon are supposed to be\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/5/995f44efd3d9791d3844223f8bd6b35c600050fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$150^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />).\n</p><p>[Solution by pf02, February 2026]\n</p><p><br />\nThe original thread for this problem can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p1768573\">[1]</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO\" title=\"1977 IMO\">1977 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1977_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1977 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017100022\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.038 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.059 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 312/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 868/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 52/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 13003/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.771      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.771      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:20518:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017100022 and revision id 277661. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}